er-fields "answer-field-08b63179-28da-5781-a56a-19cfea55072a"] [17592186083188 :knowledge-point/questions "question-8de5ec05-717f-57d0-aca0-bdf66baf299d"] ["question-7198fc6e-53bf-58ce-9c21-b096160c3a27" :question/id #uuid "7198fc6e-53bf-58ce-9c21-b096160c3a27"] ["question-7198fc6e-53bf-58ce-9c21-b096160c3a27" :question/math-academy-id "q-106550"] ["question-7198fc6e-53bf-58ce-9c21-b096160c3a27" :question/problem "![](images/9f/9fd54a6b5f5c5c4a711dbdc7de7fcea84177454d6f4a04a1fd647e6a9d93b904.png)\n\nFind the value of $k$ for the parabola $y=-3{x}^{2}+kx+5,$ shown above."] ["question-7198fc6e-53bf-58ce-9c21-b096160c3a27" :question/worked-solution "First, we will calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k.$ Then, since the graph tells us that the axis of symmetry is $x=-1,$ we will set our result equal to $-1.$ \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=-3$ and $b=k$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{k}{2(-3)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k}{(-6)} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that the axis of symmetry is $x=-1,$ so we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{6} & =-1 \\\\ k & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] ["question-7198fc6e-53bf-58ce-9c21-b096160c3a27" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-5bdc9abc-9fd1-5916-ba55-1902c71afb99" :answer-field/id #uuid "5bdc9abc-9fd1-5916-ba55-1902c71afb99"] ["answer-74a39713-92e5-576b-b789-33314d80ed36" :answer/id #uuid "74a39713-92e5-576b-b789-33314d80ed36"] ["answer-74a39713-92e5-576b-b789-33314d80ed36" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-74a39713-92e5-576b-b789-33314d80ed36" :answer/value "-2"] ["answer-176dee66-a05c-595a-9ab2-46aeebf6b277" :answer/id #uuid "176dee66-a05c-595a-9ab2-46aeebf6b277"] ["answer-176dee66-a05c-595a-9ab2-46aeebf6b277" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-176dee66-a05c-595a-9ab2-46aeebf6b277" :answer/value "-4"] ["a